From: Burke Cates <burke.cates@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NOT-FOR-UPSTREAM: arm64: dts: qcom: glymur-asus-zenbook-a16: throttle on PMH0104 temperature alarms

LAB, not for upstream as-is. pmh0104-glymur.dtsi gives the pmh0104-i0 and
pmh0104-j0 thermal zones a 95 C passive trip and a 115 C critical trip but
no cooling-maps, so the passive trip does nothing. A FurMark run on battery
(~88 W discharge) took pmh0104-i0 to its critical trip and the kernel did a
HARDWARE PROTECTION shutdown, while the GPU zones sat at 92-95 C.

Map both zones' existing passive trips to the GPU devfreq cooling device and
to the three CPU clusters (cpu0, cpu6, cpu12). Which rails PMH0104 I/J feed is
not described in the DT (they are firmware-managed), hence GPU and CPU.

The cooling devices get a lower limit. The PMIC temp alarm reports its
over-temperature stage rather than a measured temperature (37 C, then 97 C,
then 115 C critical), so after the zone crosses the 95 C passive trip its
temperature stays flat at 97 C. step_wise only raises a cooling state while
the trend is rising, so with no lower limit each device moved one state and
stayed there: GPU devfreq state 1 (1700 MHz of 1850) and cpufreq state 1 on
every cluster, which on cpu6/cpu12 is the 4800 MHz boost OPP, i.e. no cap at
all with boost off. On the first throttle step_wise clamps the target to
[lower, upper], so the lower limit sets the depth of that one step (cooling
state N is N steps below the top of the device's frequency table):

  gpu          4 -> 1185 MHz of 1850 MHz
  cpu0 (0-5)   7 -> 2630.4 MHz of 3609.6 MHz (73%)
  cpu6, cpu12  9 -> 3225.6 MHz of 4454.4 MHz (72%; the table starts with
                   the 5011.2 and 4800 MHz boost OPPs)

The upper limits stay THERMAL_NO_LIMIT. When the alarm drops below the trip
the instances are released as before. Trip temperatures and hysteresis are
unchanged; only labels on the existing passive trips and cooling-maps are
added.

Applies on top of the FixItFoundry zenbook-a16-linux series.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
Not-for-upstream: local workaround for one laptop, not reviewed for
  submission; use only for local testing.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur-asus-zenbook-a16-ux3607oa.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/glymur-asus-zenbook-a16-ux3607oa.dts
@@ -2253,3 +2253,64 @@
 		};
 	};
 };
+
+/*
+ * LAB: throttle on the PMH0104 temperature alarms. pmh0104-glymur.dtsi gives
+ * both zones a 95 C passive trip but no cooling-maps, so nothing acted on it
+ * and a FurMark run on battery went straight to the 115 C critical trip
+ * (HARDWARE PROTECTION shutdown). The trips are unchanged; only labels and
+ * cooling-maps are added. Which rails PMH0104 I/J supply is not in the DT
+ * (firmware-managed), so both zones throttle the GPU and every CPU cluster.
+ *
+ * The temp alarm reports its over-temperature stage, not a measurement
+ * (37 C, then 97 C, then 115 C critical), so once the passive trip is
+ * crossed the temperature stays flat and step_wise moves each cooling
+ * device only one state. The lower limits make that one step a useful
+ * cap (cooling state N = N steps below the top of the frequency table):
+ *   gpu    4 -> 1185 MHz of 1850
+ *   cpu0   7 -> 2630.4 MHz of 3609.6 (73%)
+ *   cpu6/
+ *   cpu12  9 -> 3225.6 MHz of 4454.4 (72%; states 0-1 are the 5011.2 and
+ *               4800 MHz boost OPPs)
+ */
+&{/thermal-zones/pmh0104-i0-thermal} {
+	trips {
+		pmh0104_i0_alert: trip0 {
+		};
+	};
+
+	cooling-maps {
+		map0 {
+			trip = <&pmh0104_i0_alert>;
+			cooling-device = <&gpu 4 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+
+		map1 {
+			trip = <&pmh0104_i0_alert>;
+			cooling-device = <&cpu0 7 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+					 <&cpu6 9 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+					 <&cpu12 9 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&{/thermal-zones/pmh0104-j0-thermal} {
+	trips {
+		pmh0104_j0_alert: trip0 {
+		};
+	};
+
+	cooling-maps {
+		map0 {
+			trip = <&pmh0104_j0_alert>;
+			cooling-device = <&gpu 4 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+
+		map1 {
+			trip = <&pmh0104_j0_alert>;
+			cooling-device = <&cpu0 7 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+					 <&cpu6 9 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>,
+					 <&cpu12 9 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+		};
+	};
+};
